{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|journalism}} {{lb|en|chiefly|US|journalism}} The [[introductory]] [[paragraph#Noun|paragraph]] or paragraphs of a [[newspaper]], or a [[news#Noun|news]] or other [[type#Noun|type]] of [[article#Noun|article]]; the [[lead#Noun_2|lead]] or [[lead-in]]. {{defdate|from mid 20th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|intro}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J. W. Click; Russell N. Baird|title=Magazine Editing and Production|edition=2nd|location=Dubuque, Iowa|publisher=[[w:McGraw Hill Education|W[illiam] C. Brown]]|year=1979|page=90|isbn=978-0-697-04329-0|passage=Readers usually see the lead picture and read its caption first, before reading the '''lede''' of the article, so the article '''lede''' should not be a repetition of the caption.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Mike Godwin|chapter=Who’s a Journalist?—II: Welcome the New Journalists on the Internet|editors=[[w:Robert Giles|Robert H. Giles]]; Robert W. Snyder|title=What’s Next?: Problems & Prospects of Journalism|location=New Brunswick, N.J.; London|publisher=w:Transaction Publishers|year=1999|year_published=2001|page=46|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Z7Eluwuvx-EC&pg=PA46|isbn=978-0-7658-0709-0|passage="How can Mr. On-line Guy learn to be a journalist if he didn't go through what I went through?" they [newspaper journalists] ask. "I needed the city editor to tell me how to write a graceful sentence, and I was a year into the job before I could craft a decent '''lede'''?"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Brian McGrory|chapter=40|title=Strangled|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Atria Publishing Group|Atria Books]]|month=February|year=2007|page=314|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/strangled00mcgr/page/314/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-7434-6368-3|passage=I was thrilled to be in possession of this nugget, which could probably take over the '''lede''' of my story. This essentially and truly implicated one of the most respected homicide detectives in Boston, all based on my initial tip.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=w:Michael Tomasky|title=Michael Tomasky’s Blog: This Morning’s Stuff You Need to Know|work=w:The Guardian|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306082201/http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/michaeltomasky/2008/oct/15/johnmccain-williamtimmons|archivedate=6 March 2016|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=15 October 2008|passage=The '''lede''' (as we spell it) story in today's NYT [''{{w|The New York Times}}''] is all about their new poll showing that [[w:John McCain|[John] McCain]] is hurting himself, not [[w:Barack Obama|[Barack] Obama]], with the attacks. {{...}} If something's the '''lede''' in the NYT, it tends to get discussed on cable TV all day, etc.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Branden Salas|chapter=Reporting for Print Media|title=Basic Concept of Journalism|location=Waltham Abbey, Essex|publisher=Ed-Tech Press|year=2018|year_published=2020|page=253|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=UejEDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA253|isbn=978-1-83947-288-6|passage=Like all forms of writing, there's no hard and fast rule about what makes a great '''lede'''. A good '''lede''' changes depending on the story you're writing. {{...}} '''Ledes''' vary wildly, but you'll start to notice patterns and, more importantly, what kinds of '''ledes''' you like and feel are effective.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Naveed Saleh|chapter=Narrative: Beginnings, Middles, and Ends|title=The Writer’s Guide to Self-editing: Essential Tips for Online and Print Publishing|location=Jefferson, N.C.|publisher=w:McFarland & Company|year=2019|section=part VII (Global Considerations)|page=225|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=coCJDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT235|isbn=978-1-4766-7159-8|passage=Here are some different types of '''ledes''': {{...}} ·&nbsp;Scenario '''ledes''' use narrative elements to describe a place of particular importance to the story. / ·&nbsp;Narrative '''ledes''' begin at a chronological beginning. {{...}} ·&nbsp;First-person anecdotal '''ledes''' begin with a relevant anecdote that involves the writer. Service and celebrity pieces often begin with first-person '''ledes'''.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The word, which has entered ordinary usage, was originally journalistic jargon. In 1990, the American author and journalist {{w|William Safire}} (1929–2009) was still able to say: “You will not find this spelling in dictionaries; it is still an insiders' variant, steadily growing in frequency of use. {{...}} Will ''lede'' break out of its insider status and find its way into general use? {{...}} To suggest this is becoming standard would be misledeing {{...}} But it has earned its place as a variant spelling, soon to overtake the original spelling for the beginning of a news article.”<ref>{{cite-journal|en|author=William Safire|authorlink=William Safire|title=On language: (HED) folo my lede (UNHED)|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/11/18/magazine/on-language-hed-folo-my-lede-unhed.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210703040610/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/11/18/magazine/on-language-hed-folo-my-lede-unhed.html|archivedate=3 July 2021|magazine={{w|The New York Times Magazine}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|The New York Times Company}}|date=18 November 1990|section=section 6|page=22|issn=0028-7822|oclc=762261046}}.</ref>

